\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The usable correlation length stays below the decision lag in every group we measured.} (a) Half-life of best-arm identity vs.\ forecast horizon, one marker per decision group ($127$; color $=$ backbone, marker $=$ dataset), log--log. The solid line is the break-even condition half-life $=H$; \emph  {no group reaches it} (the maximum is $0.35H$, TimesNet/ILI/$H{=}24$), and most sit below the $H/10$ dashed line ($109/127 = 86\%$; the remaining $18$ groups fall between $0.1H$ and $0.35H$). (b) The ratio half-life$/H$ per horizon: gray dots are groups, red is the median, falling from $0.19$ at $H{=}24$ to $0.021$ at $H{=}720$. The decline is monotone \emph  {within} each of the two horizon families ($24/36/48/60$ for ILI, $96/192/336/720$ for the rest); the small step up at $H{=}96$ is the change of family, not a reversal.}}{11}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:halflife}{{1}{11}{\textbf {The usable correlation length stays below the decision lag in every group we measured.} (a) Half-life of best-arm identity vs.\ forecast horizon, one marker per decision group ($127$; color $=$ backbone, marker $=$ dataset), log--log. The solid line is the break-even condition half-life $=H$; \emph {no group reaches it} (the maximum is $0.35H$, TimesNet/ILI/$H{=}24$), and most sit below the $H/10$ dashed line ($109/127 = 86\%$; the remaining $18$ groups fall between $0.1H$ and $0.35H$). (b) The ratio half-life$/H$ per horizon: gray dots are groups, red is the median, falling from $0.19$ at $H{=}24$ to $0.021$ at $H{=}720$. The decline is monotone \emph {within} each of the two horizon families ($24/36/48/60$ for ILI, $96/192/336/720$ for the rest); the small step up at $H{=}96$ is the change of family, not a reversal}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The same decay under three independent stratifications.} Excess-over-chance agreement of best-arm identity vs.\ absolute lag (windows), by backbone (a), dataset (b) and horizon (c). Only lags with full support across the groups in a stratum are drawn. Every curve reaches chance by lag $\approx 10^{2}$; in (c) the dotted vertical lines mark each stratum's smallest legal decision lag $H$ (same color), and every curve has already flattened at or before its own line. None of the $20$ strata is an exception.}}{11}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:decay}{{2}{11}{\textbf {The same decay under three independent stratifications.} Excess-over-chance agreement of best-arm identity vs.\ absolute lag (windows), by backbone (a), dataset (b) and horizon (c). Only lags with full support across the groups in a stratum are drawn. Every curve reaches chance by lag $\approx 10^{2}$; in (c) the dotted vertical lines mark each stratum's smallest legal decision lag $H$ (same color), and every curve has already flattened at or before its own line. None of the $20$ strata is an exception}{figure.2}{}}
